    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Lunsi, the drummer-historians of the court, trace the royal line to Tohazhie the Red Hunter, who left Tunga east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-chad">Lake Chad</a> for Zamfara and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a>, and to Naa Gbewaa, who settled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pusiga">Pusiga</a> and whose children founded Mamprugu, Nanung and, through his daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yennenga">Yennenga</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mossi">Mossi</a> kingdoms.</li><li>Naa Nyagsi (r. 1416–1432) overthrew many of the Tindaamba, the earth priests who had led the Dagomba towns, set his sons, brothers and uncles over the conquered peoples and made his capital at Yani Dabari; the surviving tindaamba kept their ritual office.</li><li>At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-adibo">Battle of Adibo</a> in 1896 about 7,000 Dagomba with bows and arrows charged a German force of 100 well-armed men under Valentin von Massow, Hans Gruner and Gaston Thierry; it was a massacre, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yendi">Yendi</a> was destroyed and the Gbewaa Palace burnt.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Dagbon's scholars of the house of Moliyili wrote in a modified Arabic script, lexically Arabic but syntactically Dagbani; their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> on law, pilgrimage, medicine and metallurgy were burned in the German invasion, and much of what survived was carried to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> in the twentieth century.</p></aside>
    </section>

