-year">1888</span><span class="rf-text">Dagbon is placed in the neutral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salaga">Salaga</a> Area, from Yeji to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yendi">Yendi</a>, agreed between the Germans and the British to forestall conflict.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1896</span><span class="rf-text">The Germans defeat the Dagomba at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-adibo">Battle of Adibo</a>, destroy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yendi">Yendi</a> and burn the Gbewaa Palace; Eastern Dagbon falls to German Togoland.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1899</span><span class="rf-text">Yaa Naa Andani II dies in August; the British and Germans split Dagbon between German Togoland and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-coast">Gold Coast</a>.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1900</span><span class="rf-text">On 7 April Friedrich Rigler's German expedition attacks the Dagomba who had fled to Sang in British territory, kills at least 83, among them Andani's son Idi, and installs Alasan as Yaa Naa.</span></li></ol>
