    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/yennenga"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yennenga.jpg" alt="Yennenga" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/yennenga">Yennenga</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yennega.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Women in African History: an E-Learning Tool; CC-BY-SA UNESCO/Brenda Gael McSweeney, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Dagbon was settled long before it was a kingdom. Archaeology shows farming and iron working in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a>, and the aboriginal Dagomba, the Dagbon Sablisi, lived in a decentralised society of towns, Tinsi, each headed by a tindana, an earth priest who pacified the tingbana, the earth gods, the bina and the buga. The royal line came from outside. The Lunsi tell of Tohazhie, the Red Hunter, who left Tunga east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-chad">Lake Chad</a> with a small band of horsemen for Zamfara in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a>, where he married Pag Wabiga, daughter of the king, and fathered Kpoginumbo. Driven south by reprisals from the rising Songhay Empire, Kpoginumbo seized power at Biun in Gurma, and his son Naa Gbewaa left Biun to settle at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pusiga">Pusiga</a> in the north-east corner of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a>.</p><p>Gbewaa unified the small states of the Tindaamba and ruled at Pusiga until he went blind. His sons Tohagu and Gmantambo founded Mamprugu and Nanung, and his daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yennenga">Yennenga</a> went north to marry Rialle, whose son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ouedraogo">Ouedraogo</a> founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mossi">Mossi</a> kingdoms. After Gbewaa's death his children under Shitobu settled briefly at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gambaga">Gambaga</a> and moved south to Namburugu near Karaga, where the king took the title Yaa Naa, 'king of strength'. Shitobu's son Naa Nyagsi (r. 1416–1432) waged a war of expansion, overthrew many of the Tindaamba, set his sons, brothers and uncles over the conquered Konkomba, Nafeba, Basare and Chamba, and made his capital at Yani Dabari near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamale">Tamale</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/kola-nut"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kola-nut.jpg" alt="Kola nut" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/kola-nut">Kola nut</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cola_acuminata_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-190.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Franz Eugen Köhler, Köhler's Medizinal-Pflanzen, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Dagbon lies in the Guinean forest–savanna mosaic, with wet and dry seasons, and holds the largest iron ore reserve in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a>. Trade made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yendi">Yendi</a>. Naa Zanjina, remembered as the first Muslim Yaa Naa, encouraged commerce, and with the capital settled at Yendi and peace restored a Muslim community grew at the palace: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wangara">Dyula</a> of Mande origin under the Sabali-Yarna and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> Muslims under the Kamshe Naa. Their trade linked Dagbon with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fezzan">Fezzan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bight-of-benin">Bight of Benin</a>, and the kingdom exchanged salt and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kola-nut">kola nuts</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mossi">Mossi</a> kingdoms, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songhai-empire">Songhai</a> empires and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa-bakwai">Hausa Bakwai</a>. By 1788 Yendi was said to be bigger than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumasi">Kumasi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salaga">Salaga</a>.</p><p>Learning was an industry of the court. The scholarly house of Moliyili, supported by the Yaa Naa with material resources and granted administrative and legal independence, wrote chronicles, biographies, jurisprudence, pilgrimage guides, Arabic grammar and Qur'anic commentary, and works on agriculture, medicine, pharmacology and metallurgy, in the Dagbanli script, a modified Arabic hand that Phyllis Ferguson describes as lexically Arabic and syntactically Dagbani; the kingdom counts Moliyili as the oldest institution of learning in Ghana. The Lunsi, the drummers under the Namo Naa, preserved history and genealogy in recited and drummed proverbs. Women held wealth and office: titles such as the Gundo Naa were reserved for them, and they ruled male subjects and owned royal lands.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The army of Dagbon is the Sapashina. Naa Nyagsi's war of expansion in 1416–1432 made the kingdom, overthrowing the earth priests and holding sway over the Konkomba, Nafeba, Basare and Chamba. Its hardest wars were with the Gonja, whose attacks on western Dagbon, Tomo, and especially on Daboya, did great damage; Naa Luro defeated them in a later battle but could not withstand the sustained warfare and moved the capital east from Yani Dabari, the 'ruins of Yani' near Diare, to the present Yani, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yendi">Yendi</a>. The Gonja followed, until Naa Zanjina decisively defeated them and killed their chief Kumpatia at Sang near Yendi.</p><p>Dagbon's well-organised and powerful army let it resist colonisation when the Germans pressed from the east, the British from the south and the French from the north. The Germans failed to take Dagbon in several attempts; from their point of view Yaa Naa Andani II had disturbed the trade road from the coast to their station at Sansanné-Mangu. In 1896 Valentin von Massow, Gruner and Gaston Thierry met the Dagomba at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-adibo">Battle of Adibo</a>: about 7,000 poorly equipped Dagomba rushed with bows and arrows at 100 well-armed Germans, and it was a massacre. Yendi was destroyed, its valuables carried off and the centuries-old Gbewaa Palace burnt. In 1900 Friedrich Rigler's expedition found Yendi deserted, crossed into British territory and on 7 April attacked Andani's son Idi and his followers at Sang, killing at least 83.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The king of Dagbon is the Yaa Naa, whose court sits at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yendi">Yendi</a>. Naa Nyagsi built the state by installing his sons, brothers and uncles as rulers over the conquered peoples, and the kingdom is divided into territorial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdoms</a> from divisional to village level; the gate chiefdoms of Karaga, Savelugu and Mion are reserved for sons of former Yaa Naas, and holding one qualifies a prince to contest the Nam at Yendi, while lesser chieftaincies go to grandsons. Succession has always rotated among the royal houses, once three, Andani Yili, Aburi Yili and Mahami Yili, now the two houses of Abudu and Andani. Governance joins the chiefs with the Tindaamba: the earth priests survived Nyagsi's conquest as ritual mediators between the people and the gods of the land, and every town still has its tindana.</p><p>Women hold chieftaincies of their own, a rarity in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>: the Gundo Naa heads all female chiefs and the Zosimli Naa makes alliances and friendships. From Naa Zanjina, the first Muslim ruler, Muslim elders entered the political structure, the Sabali-Yarna and later the Kamshe Naa saying the Yaa Naa's protective prayers, with titles such as the Walgu Naa, who saw that the king had his portion to 'drink the Qur'an', the Nayil Liman and the Yidan Kambala. Under German and then British rule the Yaa Naa remained at Yendi, but imperial interference in the succession has left the kingdom with repeated disputes.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/savannah"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/savannah.jpg" alt="Savannah" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/savannah">Savannah</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tarangire-Natpark800600.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ProfessorX, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Dagbon was a mother of kingdoms. From Naa Gbewaa's children came Mamprugu and Nanung, and through his daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yennenga">Yennenga</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mossi">Mossi</a> kingdoms of Ouagadougou, Tenkodogo, Yatenga and Fada N'Grumah, whose people are nearly half of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burkina-faso">Burkina Faso</a> and who honour her as their mother; the Bouna kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-coast">Ivory Coast</a> and the Dagaaba states of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a>'s Upper West also came out of Dagbon. At its height the kingdom covered the Northern, Upper West, Upper East, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savannah">Savannah</a> and North East regions of present-day Ghana and parts of Burkina Faso, north-eastern Ivory Coast and north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/togo">Togo</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> entered northern Ghana through its court and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wangara">Dyula</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> merchants it welcomed.</p><p>Its intellectual legacy is singular in the region: the scholarly house of Moliyili, a writing system of its own, the Dagbanli Ajami script, and centres of healing, with chieftaincies held by women. Dagbon is called the oldest and one of the most organised traditional kingdoms in Ghana, and since independence in 1957 it has held the customary role of the country's other kingdoms. The loss was also great: colonial conquest took its artefacts and its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>, the Moliyili archives burned, others shipped to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, and the partition of 1899 divided a people whose wounds, by their own account, did not fully heal until the second decade of the twenty-first century.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/first-world-war.jpg" alt="First World War" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bataille_de_Verdun_1916.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Collection DocAnciens/docpix.fr, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The scramble for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a> closed on Dagbon from three sides. In 1888 the British and Germans made a neutral zone, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salaga">Salaga</a> Area from Yeji to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yendi">Yendi</a>; in 1896 the Germans broke it at Adibo, destroyed Yendi and burnt the Gbewaa Palace, and Eastern Dagbon passed to German Togoland while Western Dagbon became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a>, not a colony, of the British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-coast">Gold Coast</a>. In 1899 the two powers formally split the kingdom, leaving the Yaa Naa at Yendi under German rule and cut off from his people in the west. When Andani II died in August 1899 the succession fell into dispute; Governor August Köhler backed Alasan, Na of Karaga, and after Rigler's troops killed at least 83 of Idi's party at Sang on 7 April 1900 Alasan was installed.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> Eastern Dagbon became British mandated territory and was reunited with the west. The British ruled through the chiefs and imposed a head tax that sent Dagomba to the southern mines and cocoa farms to earn it. Since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a>'s independence in 1957 Dagbon has been a traditional kingdom, but the imperial meddling in its succession from the 1920s left disputes that have cost lives: troops were sent to Yendi after Yaa Naa Mahama II died in 1954, and in March 2002 Yaa Naa Yakubu Andani II was murdered with forty-two of his elders in fighting by supporters of the Abudu house. A committee of three eminent chiefs settled the conflict in 2018, and on 18 January 2019 the kingmakers elected Yaa Naa Abubakari Mahama.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Kingdom of Dagbon was built by Naa Gbewaa's line in the fifteenth century, when Naa Nyagsi conquered the earth priests of the Dagomba towns. Ruled by the Yaa Naa from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yendi">Yendi</a>, it traded with Hausaland and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahel">Sahel</a>, schooled scholars at Moliyili and mothered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mossi">Mossi</a> kingdoms, until the German massacre at Adibo in 1896 and the partition of 1899.</em></p>

